---
// P10 — YouthWIDE /programs/youthwide/ (T3 conversion page; doc 04 matrices 4.1, 4.2 and 4.3; C1 → C14).
// One page for both audiences (WP4c: NationWIDE was renamed YouthWIDE and merged in). WP30d (owner direction 2026-10-14: less is more, global first): the page leads with the
// co-funded country cohorts (funders and development partners; the program is designed for South Asia, Sub-Saharan Africa and the Middle East and North Africa) and gives the Bangladesh initiative
// ONE band of its own (#governments, linked from the Governments partner page and a case study). Cut as report-like: In Short, the definition band, the priority-group cards, the
// delivery grid, the work-entry plans, the roadmap and reporting timelines, the RACI and decision sheet, the comparison table, the offer notes and the four gain rows of the C8 map.
// Kept because a gate or a registered claim needs them: the three ranked pains (C2) and one C8 row each, the positioning statement opening C6, both session agendas, the trial relevance
// qualifiers and every scope word.
// WP36 (cycle-13 reviews): "Our Approach" states what the program does (training, mentoring and placement support in existing labs and colleges, monthly reports) and no longer restates the audience and the regions the hero
// and the glance already give; scripts/check-facts.mjs declares that statement for this page (POSITION_WHOLE_STATEMENT). The Bangladesh band says "Designed for delivery through existing government digital labs
// and colleges", and the glance's Delivery row says "a central team coordinating local labs for larger programs" in place of "hub-and-spoke" (as Our Model does).
// WP39 (cycle-16 review): the hero lead is one sentence (audience, outcome and mechanism; the scope word "as agreed in the program scope" moved to the glance's Reporting row); the three-card C8 band (IncludesBand) replaces the
// funder concern / what is included / practical benefit matrix; "Choose Your Entry Point" keeps both route actions and the procurement sentence and has ONE agenda line under the two cards in place of two near-identical agendas.
// WP40 (cycle-17 review): the glance row that says where the program is designed to run is headed "Designed for Three Regions" ("Where It Runs" read as a list of countries where it runs today); the timing question uses the
// two-sentence variant (Q7-model: "training starts 6–8 weeks after signing"), because "What Happens Next" below already shows the sequence.
// WP41 (cycle-18 review): the "What Success Looks Like" band (#future) is about young people earning ("Young People Earning, Locally and Globally": jobs, freelance clients and businesses of their own); the monthly
// reports it used to describe are already in the glance's Reporting row and the C8 cards, so it no longer explains them a third time.
// WP48 (cycle-23 reviews, consistent with the owner decision of 2026-10-15 for the audience pages): the page no longer repeats Home's persuasion bands. The Cost of Waiting band ("Training Without Work Entry Leaves Budgets Without Results", #cost)
// and the Imagine band ("Young People Earning, Locally and Globally", #future) are gone, and so are the hero's two links back to audience pages ("For Development Partners", "For Governments"; the ministry briefing stays beside the one button).
// The challenge band stays, then the track record and the delivery model; the tone alternates white, airy, white, airy, Klein. The Bangladesh band's labs tile (GV-09) shows the register's qualifier as its note (lab access and seats are confirmed per program).
// WP49 (cycle-24 review): the closing paragraph under "What Happens Next" (PD-04 in full: the step names, then "training starts 6–8 weeks after signing. Public procurement follows the agency’s own timeline.") is gone: it repeated
// the step sequence above it and the timing question in the FAQ (Q7-model). The procurement sentence is still on the page, in the Governments entry card and in that answer; the risk band's eyebrow is "Managed Rollout".
// WP50 (cycle-25 review): step 4 of "What Happens Next" says "Training starts in existing labs and colleges on the agreed schedule." (it pointed at "the window below", which WP49 had removed).
// WP51 (cycle-26 review): the options lead no longer describes the website ("Program options are shown without prices; the indicative budget is shared in the discovery session."): it says "We share an indicative budget in the discovery session."
// WP52 (cycle-27 reviews, Claude): the hero's ministry-briefing text link is labelled with its audience, "Governments: Request a Briefing for Your Ministry" (the label sits outside the link, as in CTABand's `routes`; the one primary button is unchanged);
// the glance's first row says "Bangladesh is where most delivery has been" where it said "Bangladesh is the reference implementation" (IN-02 says it the same way).
// WP53 (owner follow-up, 2026-10-16, consistent with that change): the Bangladesh band was headed "Bangladesh: Delivery Base and 2030 Aim" ("Reference" was the same jargon; WP99: now "Delivery Base in Bangladesh and the 2030 Aim"), and the options band's eyebrow is "Options" (it was "Start", directly above "Three Ways to Start, ...").
// WP57 (cycle-29 review, GPT-6.1 Sol G2): the three-card C8 band ("Built Around What Funders and Agencies Ask", #features) is gone: it restated At a Glance and "One Delivery Model", so its one new detail (what evaluators check earnings against) is in the glance's
// Reporting row. The C2 pains stay (the challenge band); scripts/check-facts.mjs allows this page to carry no C8 band (PAIN_C8_OPTIONAL), and nothing links to the removed anchor. The photograph band takes the white ground the C8 band had, so white and airy still alternate after the case-study carousel.
// WP60 (cycle-32 reviews, Grok R5 and Claude S3): the "Will This Work in Our Country?" answer ends "CodersTrust has worked in 16 countries and territories; most delivery has been in Bangladesh." (it said "has worked since 2014 in 16 ...", which reads as all 16 since 2014);
// the 2030 aim carries the dependency clause wherever it is stated (AIM_DEPENDENCY, src/data/copy.ts, the clause the Programs hub card had): the "Designed for Three Regions" row says it, and the 2030 aim tile's note reads it in place of "that depends on".
// WP99 (owner answers 2026-10-28, Q1): the aim is 1 million educated youth (Higher Secondary Certificate or above) in Bangladesh and similar countries (IN-01; the row prints AIM_STATEMENT), the Bangladesh band is headed "Delivery Base in Bangladesh and the 2030 Aim" (it reached beyond Bangladesh), and the tile note says "An ambition" (it said "A national ambition"). The meta description is unchanged ("designed for three regions" is pinned, and 155 characters leave no room for "and similar countries").
// WP60 (cycle-32 review, GPT-6.1 Sol G2): the "Can You Deliver at National Scale?" answer says "Larger programs are designed to run in phases, up to 10 cohorts in parallel, with certified local facilitators." (WP148: now "..., with CodersTrust-trained facilitators; we have not yet delivered a 100,000+ program."; WP147 had "certified facilitators"; it said "We scale in phases, ...", which reads as an established configuration); the 100,000+ sentence after it is unchanged.
// WP62 (cycle-34 reviews, Sonnet and Gemini): the "Will This Work in Our Country?" answer reads "Our outcome evidence comes mainly from Bangladesh; the Kosovo pilot adds program results from outside it, and Digital Jobs Albania adds delivery experience." (Albania has no outcome figures,
// so it adds experience, not results; the answer also rests on TL-05 now); the "Where They Work" card says "... outcome tracking for 12 months after each course." (the months are PD-02's, TRACKING_MONTHS).
// WP63 (cycle-35 reviews, GPT-6.1 Sol G1 and G2, Grok R4): the glance's Reporting row says "work and earnings checked against offer letters, payslips, ..." (an offer letter documents work, not earnings); "Will This Work in Our Country?" is the bank's own Q1 now, the two-sentence answer every page prints
// ("We test local employer demand first and choose each country’s tracks with local partners. Our outcome evidence comes mainly from Bangladesh, with program results from Kosovo and delivery experience in Albania."), so the 16-countries sentence of WP60 is gone from it;
// the Managed Rollout list prints the scorecard measures in the Title Case labels Home's scorecard has. The page keeps its "Last updated" line: the program pages, the evidence pages and the legal pages carry the same line (Grok R9 asked for it to go only if no other page had one).
// WP67 (cycle-38 reviews, GLM and Sonnet): the track-record heading is "We’ve Built This Path Since 2014", as on the other pages (it said "We Have Built This Path Since 2014"; the year is ID-02's and the since-2014 rule of WP64, headingStatesSince2014, still sees it);
// the "Where They Work" card closes "..., as agreed in the program scope." (WHERE_THEY_WORK_TEXT, shared with Home and Development Partners).
// WP175 (owner decisions of 28 November 2026: every conversion page follows CMF v3, C1 to C14, in order and coverage; scratchpad wp175-decisions.md): every band sits in a Tone wrapper that carries its
// CMF component (data-cmf; tests/wp175-youthwide.spec.ts reads the order). The page runs C1 → GEO ("What Is CodersTrust?", restored; WP30d had cut it) → the glance (a recorded non-CMF row) →
// C2–C4 compressed (Q7 (a): the challenge band keeps the three ranked funder pains and ends with the C3 transition line; WP48's Cost of Waiting and Imagine bands stay off) → C5 (the empathy line, the
// one-line origin, the tiles and logos, then the method line last, Q2 (a)) → C6 (the statement first; the governments route #governments, P11, stays a C6 sub-block inside this one sequence, Q8 (a)) →
// C7 (the evidence band, moved from before the track record, Q1 (a), with WP173's trial chart; the case studies and the photographs) → C8 (restored, Q1 (a): one card per funder pain in rank order, a
// gain creator and "How It Works in Three Steps") → C9 → C10 (restored short: "What Makes CodersTrust Different", three USP lines) → C11 → C12 → C13 → C14.
import Tone from '../../components/pages/home/Tone.astro';
import DefinitionBand from '../../components/sections/DefinitionBand.astro';
import DifferenceBand from '../../components/sections/DifferenceBand.astro';
import IncludesBand from '../../components/sections/IncludesBand.astro';
import HowItWorks from '../../components/pages/partners/HowItWorks.astro';
import ProgramGlance from '../../components/pages/programs/ProgramGlance.astro';
import LandingLayout from '../../layouts/LandingLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../components/ui/Section.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../../components/ui/SectionHeader.astro';
import SessionAgenda from '../../components/ui/SessionAgenda.astro';
import EmailFallback from '../../components/ui/EmailFallback.astro';
import PhotoGallery from '../../components/ui/PhotoGallery.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/system/Hero.astro';
import Button from '../../components/system/Button.astro';
import StatusTag from '../../components/system/StatusTag.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/system/Icon.astro';
import FactStat from '../../components/facts/FactStat.astro';
import EvidenceBand from '../../components/pages/home/EvidenceBand.astro';
import CaseStudyCarousel from '../../components/sections/CaseStudyCarousel.astro';
import GainsPains from '../../components/pages/partners/GainsPains.astro';
import ProblemSection from '../../components/pages/programs/ProblemSection.astro';
import GuideSection from '../../components/pages/programs/GuideSection.astro';
import MechanismSection from '../../components/pages/programs/MechanismSection.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/pages/programs/FaqSection.astro';
import OfferSection from '../../components/pages/programs/OfferSection.astro';
import RiskSection from '../../components/pages/programs/RiskSection.astro';
import NextSteps from '../../components/pages/programs/NextSteps.astro';
import { faqNode, type FaqSource } from '../../components/pages/programs/faq';
import { KOSOVO_WOW_TILE_LABEL, WHERE_THEY_WORK_TEXT } from '../../components/pages/partners/partners';
import { C14_FIRST_FUNDERS, ctaFor, serviceNode, statValue } from '../../components/pages/programs/meta';
import { AIM_DEPENDENCY, AIM_STATEMENT, C3_TRANSITION, DELIVERY_MODEL, EMPATHY, FUNDER_PAINS, METHOD, MICRO_COMMITMENT, POSITIONING, PROGRAM_OPTIONS, PROGRAM_SCOPE_STATEMENT_EXAMS, SCORECARD_LABELS, STEPS, WB_KOSOVO, originShortUnder, painForList, painForRow, type IncludesCard } from '../../data/copy';
import { fact } from '../../data/facts';
import { galleryPhotos } from '../../data/photos';
import { formatDate, isoDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { webPage } from '../../lib/schema';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const path = '/programs/youthwide/';
const LAST_UPDATED = '2026-11-28'; // WP175 (owner decisions of 2026-11-28): CMF v3 order and coverage (the definition band, the C3 transition line, the empathy and origin lines, the evidence band after the governments band, the C8 cards and three steps, the short C10 band); Q5: the C6 positioning statement is the crafted statement for this audience (POSITIONING in src/data/copy.ts). Earlier: WP173 (owner answer of 2026-11-27): the trial comparison chart is back, 54% for the control group beside a worked-out 64% for women offered a place, with a note saying so. WP172 (owner decision 2026-11-27): the trial tiles state the published study's figures (+10 pp, +38%) and the trial comparison chart was removed. Earlier: WP166-B (marketing-effectiveness review R2, owner approval of 21 November 2026): the evidence band follows The Challenge, is headed "More Employment for Women Offered a Place, in a Randomized Trial" and shows the +41% income tile beside the +10.3 tile; the delivery-model sentence is in the "What We Deliver" card; the governments band is headed "Designed for the Digital Labs and Colleges Governments Already Have"; the safeguarding band is one FAQ answer. Earlier: WP163 (owner answers of 3 November 2026): the Training and Mentoring row names one exam fee per trainee and the AI-supported flipped classroom. Earlier: WP161 (A-38): the cases band is "Results from Earlier CodersTrust Programs" (YouthWIDE has not yet run a cohort). Earlier: WP154 (Opus Max run 1, kit 121, Minor): the "For Governments and Ministries" band ends on "Request a Briefing for Your Ministry" (the government briefing email), not the co-funding discovery-session email; the co-funding CTA (nationalFunding) is no longer used on any page. Earlier: WP151 ("Choose Your Entry Point" has no lead: the briefing-or-discovery routing is said once, in What Happens Next; the Delivery and Training rows are unchanged). Earlier: WP141 (2026-11-01: the program-scope qualifier is stated once per page). Earlier: WP129 (owner rule 2026-10-31: any change to visible text moves the date, copy edits included): the serial-comma, semicolon and copy edits of WP126 to WP128 and the WP129 edits. 29 October 2026 (WP105: the 2030 aim tile is short; WP104: the meta description names the full target group; the page's wording last changed on 28 October, WP99 and WP101). 24 October 2026 (WP86, CLR run 3 Minor 3: the Her Power wording changed that day). 21 October 2026:the survey stat OC-03 (the before-training median, $82 to $98) changed in facts.ts; WP31: the cycle-8 review wording changes
const title = 'YouthWIDE: From Training to Paid Digital Work';
// WP63 rework RW1: the hero lead and the glance row name the full group ("educated, unemployed and underemployed youth"); WP104 (Sonnet High run 1): the description names it too, in 154 characters ("trains" for "prepares"; "paid digital work" and "designed for three regions" stay). WP107 (Sonnet XHigh run 3, Polish 4): "2030 aim in Bangladesh" is "with a 2030 aim" (147 characters), so the description does not narrow the aim to Bangladesh (the aim reaches Bangladesh and similar countries, IN-01).
const description = 'YouthWIDE trains educated, unemployed and underemployed youth for paid digital work: co-funded cohorts designed for three regions, with a 2030 aim.';
const discovery = ctaFor('discovery');
const briefing = ctaFor('briefing');
const nationalSize = PROGRAM_OPTIONS[2].size.split(' ')[0];
const hours = statValue('PD-01');

// Five decision questions, one to three sentences each (cost is answered in the options band). Longer answers live on the evidence pages and in the discovery session.
const faqSources: FaqSource[] = [
  { q: 'Is YouthWIDE Right for Our Organization?', html: `Right for you if you want employment outcomes tracked after blended training delivered through existing labs and colleges. YouthWIDE is not the right fit if you need a ${nationalSize} rollout delivered by one provider in a single phase; national delivery is designed in phases.`, evidence: ['PD-05'] },
  // WP63 (cycle-35 review, GPT-6.1 Sol G2): the answer is the bank's Q1 (src/data/faqs.ts), one two-sentence answer for every page; the 16-countries sentence WP60 added here is gone with the shorter wording.
  { audience: 'all' as const, id: 'Q1' },
  {
    q: 'Can You Deliver at National Scale?',
    // WP83 (owner answer 2026-10-23): the 25,125 was worded as the national program's target ("Her Power is a national program of the Department of ICT (DoICT), under the ICT Division, to train 25,125 women ..."). WP85 (owner update 2026-10-24: the government achieved 100% of the target): "Her Power, a national program of the Department of ICT (DoICT), under the ICT Division, trained 25,125 women ..., meeting its target."
    html: `Her Power, a national program of the Department of ICT (DoICT), under the ICT Division, trained 25,125 women across 130 sub-districts (upazilas), meeting its target. CodersTrust provided the curriculum, training manuals and mentorship guidelines and trained 2,400 women directly.</p><p data-fact="PD-06 PD-05">Larger programs are designed to run in phases, up to 10 cohorts in parallel, with CodersTrust-trained facilitators; we have not yet delivered a ${nationalSize} program.`,
    evidence: ['GV-01', 'GV-11'],
  },
  { q: 'How Do You Prove Outcomes?', html: 'We plan a baseline at enrollment, follow-up surveys at 3, 6 and 12 months and monthly reports for every cohort, with documentary evidence of earnings where available. Independent evaluation is welcome.', evidence: ['PD-09', 'PD-08', 'RC-01'] },
  { audience: 'variants' as const, id: 'Q7-model' },
  // WP166-B (R2-13): the safeguarding band ("Protected by Design") is this one-line answer with its link, as on SuperKids.
  { audience: 'variants' as const, id: 'Q9-route' },
];

const jsonLd = [
  webPage({ path, name: 'YouthWIDE — From Training to Paid Digital Work', description, dateModified: LAST_UPDATED, breadcrumb: [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Programs', path: '/programs/' }, { name: 'YouthWIDE', path }] }),
  serviceNode({ path, name: 'YouthWIDE', description: fact('IN-02').text, audience: 'Ministries, agencies, development partners and funders', areaServed: ['Bangladesh', 'South Asia', 'Middle East and North Africa', 'Sub-Saharan Africa'] }),
  faqNode(faqSources, path),
];

// WP63 (cycle-35 review, Grok R4): the five scorecard measures in the Title Case labels Home's scorecard shows ("First Paid Work", "Cost per Completer and per Verified Outcome"), from one list (SCORECARD_LABELS, src/data/copy.ts).
const measures = [...SCORECARD_LABELS];

// WP175 (Q1 (a): the C8 band removed in WP57 returns in its shortest form): one card per funder pain, in the rank order of the challenge band (painForRow keeps the ids tied to FUNDER_PAINS), then one
// gain creator (doc 06 bespoke funder pain model, P10 rows). The three relievers are the cards published until WP57 (336dc4ab^), each cut to one sentence: P1 keeps its outcome sentence (its features are its
// title, and STEPS below states the work-entry plan); P2 keeps its first sentence without the scope clause (WP141), because the glance's Reporting row says what evaluators check earnings against (WP57, WP63),
// under Development Partners' heading for the same card ("Progress Your Evaluators Can Check": S10, the card is about progress reports, and "Results Your Evaluators Can Check" is a retired heading, tests/wp58);
// P3 is unchanged. The gain creator is doc 06's "Training that reaches hard-to-reach groups" (outreach and accessible selection, blended delivery in existing labs, safeguarding specified per program); doc 06's
// other gain creator, the credential, is the C10 band's credential line.
const C8_CARDS: IncludesCard[] = [
  { painId: painForRow(FUNDER_PAINS, 'P1').painId, icon: 'route', title: 'Placement Support and Mentoring', text: 'Success is measured against agreed outcomes, including paid work, not attendance alone.', facts: ['PD-15', 'PD-01', 'PD-19'] },
  { painId: painForRow(FUNDER_PAINS, 'P2').painId, icon: 'clipboard-check', title: 'Progress Your Evaluators Can Check', text: 'A baseline, monthly reports and follow-up surveys.', facts: ['PD-08', 'PD-09'] },
  { painId: painForRow(FUNDER_PAINS, 'P3').painId, icon: 'briefcase', title: 'Tracks That Follow Employer Demand', text: 'An AI-ready curriculum, with entry-level tracks set per country from employer demand, designed to keep pace as AI changes entry-level tasks.', facts: ['PD-02', 'PD-10'] },
  { icon: 'users', title: 'Training That Reaches Hard-to-Reach Groups', text: 'Outreach and accessible selection, blended delivery in existing labs and safeguarding specified per program.', facts: ['PD-14', 'PD-06', 'PD-12'] },
];
const guideTitle = `We’ve Built This Path Since ${statValue('ID-02')}`;
---

<LandingLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Programs', href: '/programs/' }, { label: 'YouthWIDE' }]} jsonLd={jsonLd} stickyCta={discovery}>
  <!-- C1 -->
  <!-- WP20c: the ministry briefing is a text link beside the one primary button (not a second button) -->
  <Tone cmf="C1">
  <div id="hero">
    <Hero
      eyebrow="YouthWIDE · Youth Digital Employment Program"
      title="From Training to Paid Digital Work, Country by Country"
      lead="YouthWIDE helps governments and funders prepare educated, unemployed and underemployed youth for paid digital work through training, mentoring and placement support."
      artSeed="youthwide"
    >
      <Fragment slot="actions">
        <Button href={discovery.href} iconEnd="arrow-right">{discovery.label}</Button>
        {
          briefing.href && (
            <nav class="routes" aria-label="Other Ways to Start">
              <span class="route">Governments: <a class="link" href={briefing.href}>{briefing.label}<Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a></span>
            </nav>
          )
        }
      </Fragment>
    </Hero>
  </div>
  </Tone>

  <!-- GEO · What is CodersTrust? (ID-01 verbatim, directly under the hero). WP175 (owner decision of 2026-11-28): restored; WP30d had cut the definition band. The glance below states the program (IN-02), so the band has no page BLUF. -->
  <Tone cmf="GEO"><DefinitionBand /></Tone>

  <!-- The glance: a recorded non-CMF row between GEO and C2 (doc 06 P10, WP175). -->
  <Tone cmf="GLANCE">
  <ProgramGlance
    title="YouthWIDE at a Glance"
    rows={[
      // WP45 (cycle-21 review): IN-01 is an initiative that AIMS to train youth by 2030, not an existing national program, so the row says "aims" (and the figure is the band's tile below, not retyped here).
      // WP48 (cycle-23 review): the row says the aim in IN-01's own wording, as the Programs hub does (WP99: AIM_STATEMENT).
      // WP166-B: the reviewer's R2-10 (put "Who It Is For" first and cut the aim sentence from this row) is not applied: the glance opens with the three regions (WP30d, the owner's global-first direction) and this row is the one place the page
      // states the aim in full (WP99, WP105: the group in brackets and the region list once; the tile below is short).
      { label: 'Designed for Three Regions', text: `South Asia, Sub-Saharan Africa and the Middle East and North Africa, as co-funded country cohorts. Bangladesh is where most delivery has been. YouthWIDE aims to train ${AIM_STATEMENT}.`, facts: ['IN-02', 'IN-01'] },
      { label: 'Who It Is For', text: 'Governments, development partners and funders who want to report on educated, unemployed and underemployed young people moving into paid digital work.' },
      { label: 'Delivery', text: 'Blended training in existing labs and colleges, with facilitators trained by CodersTrust (Training of Trainers); a central team coordinating local labs for larger programs.', facts: ['PD-06'] },
      // WP166-B (R2-08): the delivery-model sentence (DELIVERY_MODEL) is stated once, in the "What We Deliver" card of How It Works, not in this glance row.
      { label: 'Training and Mentoring', text: `${hours} training hours per certification, including certification exam preparation and one exam fee per trainee (any further attempts set per program), then three months of milestone-based mentoring.`, facts: ['PD-01', 'PD-02', 'PD-02a'] },
      { label: 'Placement Support', text: 'Placement support into local, remote and global work.', facts: ['PD-15'] },
      // WP57 (cycle-29 review, GPT-6.1 Sol G2): the verification detail of the removed C8 card ("Results Your Evaluators Can Check") is here: what earnings are checked against, where available.
      // WP63 (cycle-35 review, GPT-6.1 Sol G1): "work and earnings checked against offer letters, payslips, ..." (an offer letter documents work, not earnings; PD-09: "offer letters, payslips and platform or bank records document work and earnings").
      { label: 'Reporting', text: 'Monthly reports and follow-up surveys, with work and earnings checked against offer letters, payslips, platform logs or bank receipts where available.', facts: ['PD-08', 'PD-09'] },
    ]}
    scope={PROGRAM_SCOPE_STATEMENT_EXAMS}
  />
  </Tone>

  <!-- C2–C4, compressed (WP175, owner decision Q7 (a) of 2026-11-28; doc 04 4.1/4.3 and doc 06 P10 record it): the three ranked funder pains (P1–P3, checked by scripts/check-facts.mjs), then the C3
       transition line as the band's last line. The Cost of Waiting (C3) and Imagine (C4) bands stay off the page (WP48). -->
  <Tone cmf="C2-C4 (compressed)">
    <ProblemSection eyebrow="The Challenge" title="Education Alone Is Not Getting Young People into Work" factIds={['PX-01', 'PX-02']} transition={C3_TRANSITION}>
      <GainsPains painsHeading="What Gets in the Way" pains={FUNDER_PAINS.map(painForList)} />
    </ProblemSection>
  </Tone>

  <!-- C5. WP175 (Q2 (a)): the empathy line (doc 04 4.1 C5, [EMP] P10), the one-line origin (the band shows SC-03's tile, so the line names the regions, WP150), the tiles and the logo strip, then the method line last. -->
  <Tone cmf="C5">
    <GuideSection
      title={guideTitle}
      empathy={EMPATHY.youthwide}
      origin={originShortUnder(guideTitle, { tile: true })}
      statIds={['SC-01', 'SC-03']}
      partnerGroup={['government', 'academic', 'development']}
      method={METHOD.youthwide}
    />
  </Tone>

  <!-- C6 (the positioning statement opens the band) -->
  <Tone cmf="C6">
    <MechanismSection
      title="One Delivery Model, from Selection to Paid Work"
      positioning={POSITIONING.youthwide}
      blocks={[
        { label: 'Who We Reach', title: 'Educated, Unemployed and Underemployed Youth', text: 'Priority groups are agreed with you in co-design; women are a priority in every program.', icon: 'users' },
        { label: 'How We Reach Them', title: 'Accessible Selection, Blended Delivery', text: 'Outreach and an accessible readiness assessment, then blended training in existing labs and colleges, led by CodersTrust-trained facilitators.', icon: 'graduation-cap' }, // WP155 (Opus Max run 2, kit 122, Polish 1): the card says where and who teaches (the statement above no longer says "in existing labs and colleges"); the long form is in the Delivery row above (WP152)
        // WP166-B (R2-08): the card names the training, so it carries the page's one delivery-model sentence (it was in the glance's Training and Mentoring row).
        { label: 'What We Deliver', title: 'AI-Ready Training, Certification Preparation and Mentoring', text: `${DELIVERY_MODEL.text} Certification exam preparation by track, then three months of milestone-based mentoring.`, icon: 'award' },
        { label: 'Where They Work', title: 'Local, Remote and Global Work', text: WHERE_THEY_WORK_TEXT, icon: 'globe' },
      ]}
      factIds={['PD-14', 'PD-06', 'PD-01', 'PD-02', 'PD-15', 'IN-04']}
      link={{ label: 'How Our Model Works', href: '/our-model/' }}
    />
  </Tone>

  <!-- The Bangladesh initiative: one band (WP30d), built from the shared C6 band (eyebrow, headline, one paragraph, proof, text link). #governments is linked from the Governments partner page and a case study.
       WP45 (cycle-21 review): not headed "The National Initiative", which read as an existing national program (IN-01 is an initiative that aims to train youth by 2030); WP53: headed "Bangladesh: Delivery Base and 2030 Aim" (it said "Reference Country"); WP99 (owner answer 2026-10-28, Q1): headed "Delivery Base in Bangladesh and the 2030 Aim", because the aim reaches beyond Bangladesh. WP166-B (R2-11): headed "Designed for the Digital Labs and Colleges Governments Already Have" (the benefit, not an internal label; the page is not framed by one country).
       WP175 (owner decision Q8 (a) of 2026-11-28): this is the governments route (P11, the former NationWIDE page), folded into this page's one CMF sequence as a C6 sub-block; its briefing card is in C13 and its first step in C14. -->
  <Tone cmf="C6">
    <MechanismSection
      id="governments"
      tone="airy"
      eyebrow="For Governments and Ministries"
      title="Designed for the Digital Labs and Colleges Governments Already Have"
      lead="Designed for delivery through existing government digital labs and colleges."
      link={briefing.href ? { label: briefing.label, href: briefing.href } : undefined}
    >
      <div class="mech-stats">
        <div class="mech-target" data-reveal>
          <StatusTag status="Target" />
          <!-- WP105 (Sonnet XHigh run 1, kit 71, Minor 1): the full statement (the group in brackets, the region list) is said once, in the "Designed for Three Regions" row, so the tile is short: IN-01's value, a short label, and the ambition note that carries the dependency. -->
          <FactStat id="IN-01" label="educated youth YouthWIDE aims to train by 2030" />
          <p class="mech-target__note">An ambition, {AIM_DEPENDENCY}; not a delivered figure.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mech-stat"><FactStat id="GV-09" /></div>
        <div class="mech-stat"><StatusTag status="Delivered" /><FactStat id="GV-13" /></div>
      </div>
    </MechanismSection>
  </Tone>

  <!-- C7. WP166-B (R2-01, R2-02): the H2 states the result, the two trial results for women offered a place are side by side (RC-02's +10.3 tile and RC-01's +41% income tile, both register tiles), and the lead that
       repeated the tiles' own labels ("Trial figures are for ... Survey figures are among surveyed completers ...") is gone. WP175 (owner decisions Q1 (a) and Q3 (a) of 2026-11-28): the band follows the governments band
       (CMF order: C5 and C6 come first; WP166-B R2-01 had put it right after The Challenge) and still opens with the trial: its H2 is the trial result and its first sentence names the trial (TRIAL_SENTENCE), so no second trial
       line is added above it. -->
  <!-- WP172 (owner decision 2026-11-27): the tiles state the published study's figures (+10 pp, +38%). WP173 (owner answer of 2026-11-27): the trial comparison chart is back; the study gives the control group's
       employment (54%), not the rate for women offered a place, so the chart's 64% is the control rate plus RC-02's stat and its note says it is worked out. -->
  <Tone cmf="C7">
    <EvidenceBand
      id="proof-band"
      title="More Employment for Women Offered a Place, in a Randomized Trial"
      bandHeading="More Employment for Women Offered a Place, in a Randomized Trial"
      trialHeading="An Independent Randomized Trial"
      headlineFact="RC-01"
      chart="trial"
      chip={false}
      trialSentence
      statIds={['RC-02', 'RC-01']}
      surveyStatIds={['OC-03']}
      links={[{ label: 'Impact Survey 2026', href: '/impact/outcomes-2026/' }, { label: 'The Randomized Trial', href: '/impact/independent-evaluation/' }]}
    />
  </Tone>

  <!-- WP161 (A-38): the three cards are WSDFM, Kosovo and Her Power, earlier CodersTrust programs, not YouthWIDE cohorts (it said "Results from Delivered Programs") -->
  <Tone cmf="C7">
    <CaseStudyCarousel
      id="cases"
      eyebrow="Proof in Practice"
      title="Results from Earlier CodersTrust Programs"
      items={[
        { title: 'Women’s Skill Development for Freelancing Marketplace (WSDFM)', program: 'Commissioned by Porticus', factId: 'PR-01', href: '/impact/case-studies/wsdfm-women-freelancers/' },
        { title: 'Women in Online Work, Kosovo', program: 'World Bank Group', factId: 'PR-02', statLabel: KOSOVO_WOW_TILE_LABEL, note: WB_KOSOVO, href: '/impact/case-studies/kosovo-women-in-online-work/' },
        { title: 'Her Power', program: 'ICT Division / Department of ICT (DoICT)', factId: 'PR-05', href: '/impact/case-studies/her-power/' },
      ]}
    />
  </Tone>

  <!-- The gallery (a C7 sub-block) keeps the white ground between the airy carousel and the airy C8 band. -->
  <Tone cmf="C7"><PhotoGallery id="in-the-field" tone="white" eyebrow="In the Field" title="Photographs from the Field" lead="Training rooms, laptop handovers and agreements, from past programs." photos={galleryPhotos('youthwide')} /></Tone>

  <!-- C8. WP175 (Q1 (a)): restored (WP57 had removed it): one card per funder pain, in the challenge band's rank order (data-relieves, checked by scripts/check-facts.mjs), the gain creator, then
       "How It Works in Three Steps" (STEPS, as on the funded pages). The heading is the one published until WP57. The wrapper puts both parts on the airy ground (How It Works is a sub-band of the cards). -->
  <Tone cmf="C8" tone="airy">
    <IncludesBand title="Built Around What Funders and Agencies Ask" cards={C8_CARDS} tone="airy" />
    <HowItWorks steps={STEPS} />
  </Tone>

  <!-- C9 -->
  <Tone cmf="C9"><FaqSection sources={faqSources} title="Questions Funders and Agencies Ask" /></Tone>

  <!-- WP166-B (R2-13): the "Protected by Design" band is gone; safeguarding is one answer in the FAQ above (Q9-route, as on SuperKids), with its link to Governance and Accountability. -->

  <!-- C10. WP175 (owner decision of 2026-11-28): the short differentiation band, three USP lines from doc 04 §6 (USPs 1, 3 and 4: the independent evidence, the credential and the existing infrastructure).
       No table (the approaches table stays off every page, WP47). -->
  <Tone cmf="C10"><DifferenceBand usps={['evidence', 'credential', 'infrastructure']} /></Tone>

  <!-- C11: the three options; prices are not published, the budget comes in the discovery session -->
  <Tone cmf="C11"><OfferSection eyebrow="Options" title="Three Ways to Start, Sized to Your Goals" lead="Each phase is planned with the agency or funder on the results of the one before. We share an indicative budget in the briefing or discovery session." options={PROGRAM_OPTIONS} /></Tone>

  <!-- C12 -->
  <Tone cmf="C12">
    <RiskSection title="Start with a Pilot, Then Decide on Evidence">
      <Fragment slot="lead">
        <p data-fact="PD-11">A pilot scorecard is agreed before the first cohort. Missed thresholds trigger a corrective plan, expansion is your decision and independent evaluation is welcome.</p>
        <ul class="measures" role="list" aria-label="What the scorecard measures">
          {measures.map((m) => <li><Icon name="circle-check" size={18} />{m}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </Fragment>
    </RiskSection>
  </Tone>

  <!-- C13 + C14 (WP24c): ONE "Choose Your Entry Point" chooser (the funders' discovery card holds the page's one primary button; the Bangladesh briefing for
       governments is a text link in its own card, so two primary buttons never stand in one view) and ONE shared "What Happens Next".
       The cards keep the ids #cta and #cta-governments. WP39 (cycle-16 review): the two near-identical session agendas (one in each card) are ONE line under both cards; scripts/check-facts.mjs pins it for the band (#entry-point). -->
  <Tone cmf="C13">
    <Section id="entry-point" tone="airy" aria-labelledby="entry-point-h">
      <SectionHeader id="entry-point-h" eyebrow="Get Started" title="Choose Your Entry Point" />
      <div class="entry">
        {
          discovery.href && (
            <section id="cta" class="entry__card" aria-labelledby="cta-h" data-reveal>
              <h3 id="cta-h" class="t-heading-l entry__title">Funders: Talk to Us About a First Cohort</h3>
              <div class="entry__actions">
                <Button href={discovery.href} iconEnd="arrow-right">{discovery.label}</Button>
                <a class="link" href={url('/impact/')}>Read the Evidence</a>
              </div>
              {discovery.href.startsWith('mailto:') && <EmailFallback href={discovery.href} />}
              <p class="entry__micro">{MICRO_COMMITMENT}</p>
            </section>
          )
        }
        {
          briefing.href && (
            <section id="cta-governments" class="entry__card" aria-labelledby="cta-governments-h" data-reveal>
              <h3 id="cta-governments-h" class="t-heading-l entry__title">Governments: Start with a Briefing</h3>
              <p class="entry__text">Public procurement follows the agency’s own timeline, so we start with a briefing that fits it.</p>
              <p class="entry__actions">
                <a class="link" href={briefing.href}>{briefing.label}<Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a>
              </p>
              {briefing.href.startsWith('mailto:') && <EmailFallback href={briefing.href} />}
            </section>
          )
        }
      </div>
      <div class="entry__agenda"><SessionAgenda lead="shared" /></div>
    </Section>
  </Tone>

  <Tone cmf="C14">
    <NextSteps
      steps={[
        { title: 'Briefing or Discovery', text: 'Governments start with a briefing; funders start with a discovery session.' },
        { title: 'Co-Design', text: 'Priority groups, tracks, labs, safeguarding and the scorecard are shaped together with you.' },
        { title: 'Agreement', text: 'Scope, schedule and the pilot scorecard are agreed, on the agency’s own timeline where it applies.' },
        { title: 'First Cohort', text: 'Training starts in existing labs and colleges on the agreed schedule.' },
        { title: 'Monthly Reports', text: 'Reports are designed to show enrollment, completion, certification, placement and first earnings.' },
      ]}
      roleActions={[
        // WP155 (Opus Max run 2, kit 122, Polish 7): the Governments closing line is gone; the entry-point band and Step 1 say "we start with a briefing". The Funders line is the page's one request for two suitable times.
        { role: 'Funders', text: C14_FIRST_FUNDERS },
      ]}
    />
  </Tone>

  <!-- WP175: not a CMF band; the wrapper keeps the 32px join to a band on the same ground above it (Tone's join rule reads wrappers, not bare bands). -->
  <Tone>
    <Section pad="sm" aria-label="Last updated">
      <p class="updated">Last updated <time datetime={isoDate(LAST_UPDATED)}>{formatDate(LAST_UPDATED)}</time></p>
    </Section>
  </Tone>
</LandingLayout>

<style>
  .routes {
    flex-basis: 100%;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-xs) var(--space-lg);
  }
  /* The Bangladesh band's proof: the 2030 aim (with its "Target" label and fact) beside two figures; one column on a phone, three from 900 px.
     WP101 widened the aim column for the register's full label; WP105 gives the tile a short label, so the three columns are equal again and the label keeps a 60-character measure. */
  .mech-stats {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .mech-stats {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
  .mech-target :global(.stat__label) {
    max-width: 60ch;
  }
  /* WP50 (owner design rule 2026-10-16): no left-edge accent bar on the 2030 aim (it was a 4 px tertiary border with a left inset); its "Target" tag and note still set it apart. */
  .mech-target {
    padding: var(--space-xs) 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    justify-items: start;
  }
  .mech-target > :global(*) {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .mech-target__note {
    font-size: var(--fs-body-xs);
    color: var(--on-surface-variant);
  }
  .mech-stat {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    justify-items: start;
    align-content: start;
  }
  /* Choose Your Entry Point (WP24c): two cards, funders and governments; WP39: one agenda line under both (it replaced two near-identical agendas, one in each card) */
  .entry {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .entry {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
  .entry__card {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    align-content: start;
    padding: var(--space-md);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--primary-fixed);
    border-radius: var(--radius-default);
    color: var(--on-surface);
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }
  .entry__card > :global(*) {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .entry__title {
    color: var(--secondary);
  }
  .entry__text,
  .entry__micro {
    color: var(--on-surface-variant);
  }
  .entry__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-sm) var(--space-lg);
  }
  /* the shared agenda line sits under both cards, across their full width */
  .entry__agenda :global(.agenda) {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    max-width: none;
  }
  .measures {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .measures li {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    align-items: center;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--primary-fixed);
    color: var(--on-primary-fixed);
    font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
    font-size: var(--fs-body-base);
  }
  .updated {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    font-size: var(--fs-body-xs);
    color: var(--on-surface-variant);
  }
</style>
